        ":activity/id": "1129f5af-8b20-5759-9f3e-97b087634b1b",
        ":activity/steps": [
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186134801,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186134929,
              ":tutorial/content": "**Periodic functions** are special functions that repeat themselves every $c$ units. The smallest positive value of $c$ that has this property is called the **period** of the function.\n\nLet's take the following periodic function as an example:\n\n![](image_assets/297884a427a047f5b0209ac6933e06b2-f483c51678.png)\n\nThis function repeats itself with a period of $2$. To illustrate, first focus on the part of the graph shown in blue below.\n\n![](image_assets/f553c854f69e02657b402b40469a7078-fbba2663aa.png)\n\nIf we shift this part $2$ units to the right, the graph remains unchanged!\n\n![](image_assets/90b02afb5183c653fe9bc0e6bff04426-deeadebca7.png)\n\nThe same would happen if we shifted the part $2$ units to the left.\n\nBy knowing the period of a periodic function, we can use a value of the function to infer many other values of the function. For example, in the function above, note that\n\n$$\nf(4)=0\n$$\n\nSince the period is $2$, if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $2$ from the input, the output stays the same. We can write this as\n\n$$\nf(4+2n) = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nFor example, setting\n\n$$\nn=1,2,3\\ldots\n$$\n\ngives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn = 1: f(4 + 2(1)) &= f(6) = 0 \\\\\nn = 2: f(4 + 2(2)) &= f(8) = 0 \\\\\nn = 3: f(4 + 2(3)) &= f(10) = 0 \\\\\n&= \u22ee\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe same is true for negative values of $n$. Setting\n\n$$\nn=-1,-2,-3\\ldots\n$$\n\ngives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn =-1: f(4 + 2(-1)) &= f(2) = 0 \\\\\nn =-2: f(4 + 2(-2)) &= f(0) = 0 \\\\\nn =-3: f(4 + 2(-3)) &= f(-2) = 0 \\\\\n&= \u22ee\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
              ":tutorial/id": "b8020c2e-cfe4-5187-94df-23d7d0fcb605",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 1502,
              ":tutorial/title": "Introduction"
            },
